        "date_published": "2026-11-07T15:14:40+00:00",
        "description": "<p>I love open-source software and open protocols, but my family and friends primarily communicate through SMS for exactly the reasons in https://xkcd.com/2365/<p>Whenever XMPP or Matrix threads pop up on HN, several commenters casually mention their \"friends and family XMPP (or Matrix) server.\"<p>How did you convince friends and family to move away from SMS/WhatsApp and replace it with a niche messaging app?<p>My family and friends aren't hardcore nerdy, but they're more tech-savvy than average. Their needs in messaging are mainly pragmatic: they want ease of use, reliability, and fun features. They don't care that much about privacy, decentralization, or extensibility (though I care about those things a lot).<p>I've been experimenting with self-hosting XMPP and Matrix servers and clients in the past week, and the UX is even worse than I expected. It seems like all the clients either imitate Slack, imitate Discord, or imitate hot garbage. I haven't seen any that try to be a simple, f",
